## Session 51 summary

**Criteria NOT met** — `posix_semantics_multi16` still fails (the marker was not written).

### What happened
The original handoff blocker (cross_write_read torn-dir-444 shutdown) turned out to be an **infra** problem, not a code one. The bulk of this session was recovering an SCST/iSCSI kernel wedge, then discovering the test landscape has moved well past where the handoff thought it was.

**1. Built `5D6E7445`** — added a `P-WRBUF-DIRTORN` write-side torn-dinode detector to `pal/linux/xfs_buf.c` (the authoritative "about to hit disk" point), complementing the iflush-copy-in probe from sess50.

**2. Recovered the SCST CAW↔READ wedge (proven, no host reboot).** disk1 had a stuck "registrants-only" PR reservation that `sg_persist --clear` couldn't clear (register wouldn't stick). Root: a **CAW↔READ atomic deadlock** parking all commands. My `del_device` made it worse (wedged the SCST sysfs work thread + 49 D-state `iscsi_conn_cleanup`). The fix: gdb on `/proc/kcore` via scst_vdisk's `vdev_list` → found the exact CAW (`0x…f23ac00`) and back-edge READ (`0x…f23f0c0`) → `scst_unwedge.ko` broke the edge → instant full cascade recovery → recreated `disk1` (clean PR) → remapped 16 LUNs → re-logged-in host sessions. Full exact procedure saved to memory.

**3. Ran the 16-node cluster phase clean.** Results:
- **PASS (7):** concurrent_mkdir, concurrent_touch, concurrent_write, cross_visibility, **cross_write_read (9s — the former blocker, now solved)**, cv_disc, large_file_integrity.
- **FAIL — new blockers:** `test_dir_stress` (19/80 assertions, 30s — real correctness bug), `test_discovery` (~125s/node — slowness ≈ 2× lease timeout). `test_rename_vis_dbg` may also be slow/stuck.

### Next session
Attack `test_dir_stress` first (correctness, fast, tractable): read `tests/cluster/test_dir_stress.sh`, find which 19 assertions fail, get per-node dmesg. Then `test_discovery`'s 125s slowness. The cluster is up on `5D6E7445`; if dirty, `scripts/cluster_reset_n.sh 16` (prep sequentially if it reports NOT_LOADED). The SCST recovery procedure is in memory `sess51-scst-caw-read-wedge-full-recovery-proven` if the wedge recurs.

